select public.prepify_load_bank('Chemistry', 8, $j$[["A student mixes hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide in a beaker. The beaker becomes warm. This observation indicates that the reaction is:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Reversible","Photochemical"],"B",5,1,"b094ac85cd6e",1],["During an experiment, a student notices that a reaction vessel becomes colder than its surroundings. The reaction is most likely:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Catalytic","Neutral"],"B",5,1,"439adc82cefb",1],["A chemist studies a reaction occurring inside a flask. According to energetics, the reacting chemicals inside the flask represent the:",["Surroundings","Environment","System","Catalyst"],"C",5,1,"31e16d27385b",1],["A fuel burns in a furnace and releases large amounts of heat. The sign of ΔH for this process is:",["Positive","Zero","Negative","Infinite"],"C",5,1,"03b08dcfcb7e",1],["A student claims that energy is released when chemical bonds are broken. Which statement is correct?",["Student is correct","Bond breaking absorbs energy","Bond breaking and formation both release energy","Energy is not involved in bonding"],"B",5,1,"ed937d89ad31",1],["Two reactions occur under identical conditions. Reaction X requires 50 kJ activation energy while Reaction Y requires 120 kJ. Which reaction is expected to occur more readily?",["X","Y","Both equally","Cannot be predicted"],"A",5,1,"f97513b1986e",1],["A catalyst is added to a reaction mixture. Which change is expected?",["Increase in ΔH","Increase in activation energy","Faster reaction rate","More products formed than predicted"],"C",5,1,"21019bb5ac38",1],["A reaction has ΔH = +75 kJ. Which statement is correct?",["Heat is released","Heat is absorbed","No energy change occurs","Reaction is exothermic"],"B",5,1,"a4b4bc8ee185",1],["A student observes that ice melts when left on a laboratory bench. The process is:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Catalytic","Oxidative"],"B",5,1,"8b29df0a6aa0",1],["A spark is needed to start the combustion of petrol even though combustion is exothermic. The spark provides:",["Enthalpy change","Bond energy","Activation energy","Kinetic energy of products"],"C",5,1,"bf1691c88910",1],["A reaction proceeds only when particles collide with sufficient energy and correct orientation. This idea is explained by:",["Atomic theory","Collision theory","Kinetic theory","Molecular theory"],"B",5,1,"0081eb19ab34",1],["A student heats calcium carbonate strongly. The decomposition occurs because thermal energy:",["Forms stronger bonds","Supplies activation energy","Reduces mass","Increases pressure only"],"B",5,1,"15a14b25e377",1],["A reaction pathway changes after adding a catalyst. However, the total energy released remains unchanged. This shows that catalysts:",["Change ΔH","Lower activation energy only","Increase product mass","Alter reactants permanently"],"B",5,1,"045755d14d8f",1],["A chemical engineer wants to maximize the number of effective collisions. Which factor is most important?",["Color of reactants","Correct orientation of particles","Shape of container","Type of thermometer used"],"B",5,1,"355828a0ec3a",1],["A student compares two reactions. One has ΔH = –150 kJ and the other ΔH = –50 kJ. Which releases more heat?",["First reaction","Second reaction","Both equal","Cannot be compared"],"A",5,1,"8ffc8fdf1299",1],["In a laboratory, ammonium nitrate dissolves in water and the solution becomes cold. This indicates that:",["Heat is released","Heat is absorbed","No energy transfer occurs","Catalyst is present"],"B",5,1,"ff386a5777d8",1],["A reaction does not occur despite frequent collisions between particles. The most likely reason is:",["Collisions lack sufficient energy","Reactants have mass","Products are unstable","System is closed"],"A",5,1,"374564a6d0c2",1],["A scientist records a negative ΔH value for a reaction. This means:",["Products contain more energy than reactants","Heat is absorbed","Heat is released","Activation energy is zero"],"C",5,1,"474dadaaab48",1],["A student performs an experiment inside a calorimeter. The calorimeter itself is considered:",["System","Product","Surroundings","Reactant"],"C",5,1,"34ed917afe1f",1],["Which process requires continuous absorption of energy?",["Freezing water","Condensation of steam","Melting ice","Formation of water from hydrogen and oxygen"],"C",5,1,"39cada73e792",1],["A reaction releases 250 kJ of energy. Which value best represents ΔH?",["+250 kJ","–250 kJ","0 kJ","+125 kJ"],"B",5,1,"9a233a42b1b0",1],["A catalyst is recovered unchanged after a reaction. This demonstrates that catalysts:",["Are reactants","Are consumed completely","Remain chemically unchanged","Become products"],"C",5,1,"e26031d472f7",1],["A reaction with a very high activation energy is usually:",["Faster","Slower","More exothermic","More reversible"],"B",5,1,"37fe3ac04a81",1],["When hydrogen burns in oxygen, water is formed and heat is released. Which statement is correct?",["Bond breaking releases energy only","Bond formation releases energy","No bonds are formed","Heat is absorbed overall"],"B",5,1,"122e5e669a14",1],["A student accidentally removes the catalyst from an industrial process. The most likely consequence is:",["Reaction stops completely","Reaction becomes faster","Reaction becomes slower","ΔH becomes positive"],"C",5,1,"c6822fd575ec",1],["Which observation provides direct evidence that a reaction is exothermic?",["Temperature of surroundings increases","Temperature decreases","Color changes","Gas is produced"],"A",5,1,"88add2202c6f",1],["A reaction absorbs 120 kJ from surroundings. The products will generally possess:",["Less energy than reactants","Equal energy to reactants","More energy than reactants","No measurable energy"],"C",5,1,"afd32a2efe02",1],["A student observes many collisions but very little product formation. Which factor is most likely missing?",["Correct orientation","Reactants","Container","Mass"],"A",5,1,"ac8b3bc829cd",1],["Which situation best represents an activated complex?",["Stable product","Highly unstable intermediate state","Catalyst particle","Final reactant mixture"],"B",5,1,"7d456a4954e0",1],["An industrial chemist wants to increase production without changing ΔH. The best option is:",["Add catalyst","Change product formula","Increase product mass","Change reaction type"],"A",5,1,"ec0295d976d8",1],["A reaction profile diagram shows a very tall energy barrier. This barrier represents:",["ΔH","Potential energy of products","Activation energy","Heat released"],"C",5,1,"f1d429081b62",1],["A process releases heat while converting liquid water into ice. The process is:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Catalytic","Neutral"],"B",5,1,"5f31cbf100ec",1],["A student touches the outside of a reaction vessel and finds it hot. Which conclusion is valid?",["Reaction absorbs heat","Heat flows from surroundings to system","Heat flows from system to surroundings","No energy transfer occurs"],"C",5,1,"7ec987b9f606",1],["Why is a matchstick rubbed before it ignites?",["To decrease activation energy","To supply activation energy","To increase ΔH","To reduce bond energy"],"B",5,1,"4b059574dc80",1],["A reaction proceeds rapidly after catalyst addition because:",["More products are created instantly","Activation energy decreases","ΔH becomes zero","Bond energies increase"],"B",5,1,"56fd44fafe22",1],["During respiration, glucose reacts with oxygen. The released energy is mainly used for:",["Producing catalysts","Life processes","Destroying enzymes","Increasing atomic mass"],"B",5,1,"057852d92c7d",1],["A student compares two exothermic reactions. Reaction A has ΔH = –80 kJ and Reaction B has ΔH = –200 kJ. Which releases more energy?",["Reaction A","Reaction B","Both equal","Cannot determine"],"B",5,1,"c0cbd8097474",1],["The decomposition of limestone requires continuous heating. Therefore, the reaction is:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Catalytic","Neutralization"],"B",5,1,"c83aef2f4d20",1],["A catalyst provides an alternative pathway that is:",["Longer and slower","Higher in energy","Lower in activation energy","Independent of reactants"],"C",5,1,"cc613ee7e6d5",1],["A student records a positive ΔH value. Which statement is true?",["Heat leaves the system","Heat enters the system","Reaction is exothermic","Catalyst is absent"],"B",5,1,"b71853a99963",1],["Which situation best illustrates energy absorption during bond breaking?",["Formation of H₂O from H₂ and O₂","Ignition of methane","Separation of atoms in a molecule","Freezing of water"],"C",5,1,"9aea7d78528c",1],["An effective collision occurs when particles:",["Move slowly","Have sufficient energy and proper orientation","Are large in size","Contain catalyst atoms"],"B",5,1,"c3ab6e09622d",1],["Which process is most likely to have a positive ΔH?",["Condensation of steam","Freezing water","Burning coal","Thermal decomposition of limestone"],"D",5,1,"d290aa3c125e",1],["A catalyst is present in a reaction vessel. At the end of the experiment, its mass remains unchanged. This confirms that catalysts:",["Are consumed and regenerated","Remain unchanged overall","Become products","Are destroyed slowly"],"B",5,1,"4e735afb7ba6",1],["A student wants to identify an exothermic reaction. Which evidence is most reliable?",["Heat is evolved","Gas is formed","Color changes","Precipitate forms"],"A",5,1,"7f01abffe4bf",1],["A reaction has low activation energy. Under identical conditions, it will generally:",["Proceed more easily","Require more catalyst","Absorb more heat","Have positive ΔH"],"A",5,1,"3241d39447a1",1],["In a reaction profile, the difference between reactant energy and peak energy represents:",["ΔH","Activation energy","Heat of formation","Bond energy"],"B",5,1,"cffdd74d92d0",1],["A chemical reaction releases energy because:",["More energy is absorbed in bond breaking than released in bond formation","Energy changes are random","More energy is released in bond formation than absorbed in bond breaking","No bonds are involved"],"C",5,1,"f013085fe5c5",1],["A student is asked to identify the main purpose of studying chemical energetics. The best answer is:",["To calculate atomic masses only","To understand energy changes in chemical reactions","To classify elements only","To determine molecular shapes only"],"B",5,1,"ceeae7b128ee",1],["The study of energy changes during chemical reactions is called:",["Electrochemistry","Thermochemistry","Chemical Energetics","Organic Chemistry"],"C",1,0,"6bda3f9f7387",0],["In a chemical reaction, energy is exchanged between:",["Atoms and molecules","System and surroundings","Reactants and catalyst","Products and catalyst"],"B",1,0,"3892301559cc",1],["The part of the universe under study is called:",["Environment","Surroundings","System","Medium"],"C",1,0,"3a7b1feb2f86",1],["Everything outside the system is called:",["Catalyst","Medium","Reactant","Surroundings"],"D",1,0,"7bfc37971c8d",1],["Breaking a chemical bond always:",["Releases energy","Consumes energy","Produces light only","Produces sound only"],"B",1,0,"6ecf69390c57",1],["Formation of a chemical bond always:",["Absorbs energy","Consumes heat","Releases energy","Stops reaction"],"C",1,0,"d756f6aad0b0",1],["A reaction that releases heat to the surroundings is:",["Endothermic","Neutral","Reversible","Exothermic"],"D",1,0,"59e57d6cdaf9",1],["Combustion of methane is an example of:",["Endothermic reaction","Exothermic reaction","Decomposition reaction","Neutralization reaction"],"B",1,0,"e5f5ce17c280",1],["In an exothermic reaction, heat flows:",["From surroundings to system","From system to surroundings","Into catalyst only","Into reactants only"],"B",1,0,"c0ca7a5a549b",1],["A reaction that absorbs heat from its surroundings is:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Displacement","Redox"],"B",1,0,"c3cb53007819",1],["Which process is endothermic?",["Freezing water","Condensation of steam","Sublimation of dry ice","Combustion of fuel"],"C",1,0,"dac93682d003",1],["The thermal energy evolved or absorbed during a reaction is called:",["Entropy","Activation energy","Enthalpy of reaction","Kinetic energy"],"C",1,0,"0dc363ec040f",1],["The symbol used for enthalpy change is:",["ΔE","ΔH","ΔG","ΔS"],"B",1,0,"542c4432e0c2",1],["For an exothermic reaction, ΔH is:",["Positive","Zero","Negative","Infinite"],"C",1,0,"d6cdfe81d58f",1],["For an endothermic reaction, ΔH is:",["Negative","Positive","Zero","Undefined"],"B",1,0,"1b3a2a44960c",1],["Standard enthalpy change is measured at:",["0 K","100°C","298 K","500 K"],"C",1,0,"eff3c383cea5",1],["The energy required to break one mole of a bond is called:",["Bond formation energy","Activation energy","Bond dissociation energy","Heat of combustion"],"C",1,0,"e0e0d4c747b9",1],["The energy released when one mole of bonds is formed is:",["Bond formation energy","Activation energy","Ionization energy","Bond dissociation energy"],"A",1,0,"64e2ce73de80",1],["The enthalpy change of a reaction depends upon:",["Bond energies involved","Color of reactants","Shape of container","Pressure only"],"A",1,0,"46468f8c6a0b",1],["A reaction with ΔH = –393.5 kJ is:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Reversible","Slow"],"B",1,0,"464be1c64848",1],["The minimum energy required for effective collision is:",["Potential energy","Enthalpy","Activation energy","Nuclear energy"],"C",1,0,"73d291448efa",1],["A reaction will occur only when particles:",["Have mass","Possess activation energy","Are colored","Are liquids"],"B",1,0,"5b7230a9796a",1],["According to collision theory, a reaction occurs when particles:",["Dissolve completely","Collide effectively","Become ions","Change color"],"B",1,0,"9b3fdbfb362f",1],["An effective collision must have:",["Proper orientation and sufficient energy","High pressure only","Large molecules only","Catalyst only"],"A",1,0,"32125fc6a649",1],["If reacting particles possess energy less than activation energy:",["Reaction speeds up","Reaction becomes exothermic","No reaction occurs","Products form instantly"],"C",1,0,"b42c20f6cd6a",1],["Higher activation energy generally means:",["Faster reaction","Slower reaction","No effect on rate","More products"],"B",1,0,"bbf4911f66fd",1],["A catalyst increases reaction rate by:",["Increasing ΔH","Raising temperature permanently","Lowering activation energy","Increasing product mass"],"C",1,0,"078b9fc35eea",1],["A catalyst remains:",["Consumed completely","Chemically unchanged","Converted into product","Burnt away"],"B",1,0,"ca56aa400d9b",1],["The phenomenon of using a catalyst is called:",["Catalysis","Neutralization","Distillation","Sublimation"],"A",1,0,"a72b1cb8cd4e",1],["A catalyst affects:",["Activation energy","Enthalpy change","Mass of products","Nature of reactants"],"A",1,0,"c6ee16420f3b",1],["A catalyst does NOT change:",["Reaction pathway","Activation energy","Enthalpy change","Reaction rate"],"C",1,0,"5e8581e965af",1],["Enzymes are catalysts found in:",["Metals","Living organisms","Rocks","Fuels"],"B",1,0,"dcd577d2c6f7",1],["Respiration is a process in which:",["Energy is absorbed from surroundings","Energy is released from food","Water is produced only","Oxygen is formed"],"B",1,0,"ae87e550f87a",1],["Respiration is generally:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Neutral","Photochemical"],"B",1,0,"5e9c0194a77a",1],["The main source of energy in respiration is:",["Nitrogen","Glucose","Carbon dioxide","Water"],"B",1,0,"429a00a4af20",1],["Which process releases heat?",["Melting ice","Sublimation of dry ice","Combustion of fuel","Decomposition of limestone"],"C",1,0,"c712ad2a10dd",1],["A student studies limestone reacting with hydrochloric acid in a test tube. The reacting chemicals represent the:",["Surroundings","Environment","System","Catalyst"],"C",1,1,"b91626be76a2",1],["The test tube and air around it, are considered:",["Reactants","System","Products","Surroundings"],"D",1,0,"0395c2d029d2",1],["Which process is exothermic?",["Freezing water","Sublimation of dry ice","Thermal decomposition of limestone","Breaking bonds"],"A",1,0,"ef05e3e50509",1],["During an endothermic reaction:",["Heat enters the system","Heat leaves the system","No energy transfer occurs","Catalyst absorbs all heat"],"A",1,1,"e32eb9e52a09",1],["The combustion of carbon producing carbon dioxide has:",["Positive ΔH","Negative ΔH","Zero ΔH","Variable ΔH"],"B",1,0,"040bea455e58",1],["The reaction H₂ + I₂ → 2HI has ΔH = +53.8 kJ. It is:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Neutral","Catalytic"],"B",1,0,"a7e49850f991",1],["Which statement is correct?",["Bond breaking releases energy.","Bond formation absorbs energy.","Bond breaking absorbs energy.","Neither bond breaking nor formation involves energy."],"C",1,0,"af78715bcffc",1],["The activated complex forms during:",["Effective collision","Condensation","Evaporation","Crystallization"],"A",1,0,"e9f0655a6b2e",1],["A reaction pathway with lower activation energy is provided by:",["Heat only","Pressure only","Catalyst","Solvent only"],"C",1,0,"6bf7d99caa2b",1],["Which quantity determines whether a reaction is exothermic or endothermic?",["Color change","ΔH value","Number of atoms","Volume of container"],"B",1,0,"eeae5f513a9c",1],["If energy released during bond formation is greater than energy required for bond breaking, the reaction is:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Reversible","Neutral"],"B",1,0,"ce33384fb23d",1],["A chemical equation showing heat change is called:",["Ionic equation","Balanced equation","Thermochemical equation","Molecular equation"],"C",1,0,"fbd03672ebb2",1],["Which factor is essential for successful collision?",["Proper orientation","Large volume","High density only","Presence of water"],"A",1,0,"09a293a9bffa",1],["The overall effect of a catalyst on a reaction is to:",["Increase activation energy","Slow down reaction permanently","Increase reaction rate","Change products formed"],"C",1,0,"6db38bb93a00",1],["Burning 1 mol of methane releases 890 kJ of heat. How much heat is released when 2 mol burn completely?",["445 kJ","890 kJ","892 kJ","1780 kJ"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-scale-enthalpy",0],["Burning 1 mol of carbon releases 393 kJ of heat. How much heat is released when 2 mol burn completely?",["196.5 kJ","786 kJ","393 kJ","395 kJ"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-scale-enthalpy",0],["Burning 1 mol of methane releases 890 kJ of heat. How much heat is released when 3 mol burn completely?",["296.7 kJ","890 kJ","2670 kJ","893 kJ"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-scale-enthalpy",0],["Burning 1 mol of ethanol releases 1367 kJ of heat. How much heat is released when 3 mol burn completely?",["455.7 kJ","1367 kJ","1370 kJ","4101 kJ"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-scale-enthalpy",0],["Burning 1 mol of ethanol releases 1367 kJ of heat. How much heat is released when 2 mol burn completely?",["683.5 kJ","2734 kJ","1369 kJ","1367 kJ"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-scale-enthalpy",0],["Burning 1 mol of hydrogen releases 286 kJ of heat. How much heat is released when 0.5 mol burn completely?",["143 kJ","572 kJ","286.5 kJ","286 kJ"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-scale-enthalpy",0],["The reactants of a reaction have 510 kJ of energy and the products have 660 kJ. Which statement is correct?",["ΔH = +150 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −150 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = −150 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +150 kJ; exothermic"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-enthalpy-sign",0],["The reactants of a reaction have 530 kJ of energy and the products have 690 kJ. Which statement is correct?",["ΔH = −160 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −160 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +160 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +160 kJ; endothermic"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-enthalpy-sign",0],["The reactants of a reaction have 170 kJ of energy and the products have 210 kJ. Which statement is correct?",["ΔH = −40 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −40 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +40 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +40 kJ; exothermic"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-enthalpy-sign",0],["The reactants of a reaction have 200 kJ of energy and the products have 310 kJ. Which statement is correct?",["ΔH = −110 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +110 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +110 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −110 kJ; endothermic"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-enthalpy-sign",0],["The reactants of a reaction have 400 kJ of energy and the products have 500 kJ. Which statement is correct?",["ΔH = −100 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −100 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +100 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +100 kJ; exothermic"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-enthalpy-sign",0],["The reactants of a reaction have 570 kJ of energy and the products have 520 kJ. Which statement is correct?",["ΔH = −50 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = −50 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +50 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +50 kJ; exothermic"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-enthalpy-sign",0],["In a reaction, 1800 kJ of energy is absorbed to break the bonds of the reactants and 1550 kJ is released when the bonds of the products form. Which is correct?",["ΔH = +250 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +250 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +3350 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −250 kJ; exothermic"],"A",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-bond-energy",0],["In a reaction, 800 kJ of energy is absorbed to break the bonds of the reactants and 750 kJ is released when the bonds of the products form. Which is correct?",["ΔH = +50 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +50 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −50 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +1550 kJ; endothermic"],"B",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-bond-energy",0],["In a reaction, 1300 kJ of energy is absorbed to break the bonds of the reactants and 1000 kJ is released when the bonds of the products form. Which is correct?",["ΔH = +300 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = −300 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +300 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +2300 kJ; endothermic"],"C",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-bond-energy",0],["In a reaction, 1050 kJ of energy is absorbed to break the bonds of the reactants and 1200 kJ is released when the bonds of the products form. Which is correct?",["ΔH = +150 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −150 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +2250 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −150 kJ; exothermic"],"D",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-bond-energy",0],["In a reaction, 1750 kJ of energy is absorbed to break the bonds of the reactants and 1450 kJ is released when the bonds of the products form. Which is correct?",["ΔH = +300 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +300 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +3200 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = −300 kJ; exothermic"],"A",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-bond-energy",0],["In a reaction, 1100 kJ of energy is absorbed to break the bonds of the reactants and 1050 kJ is released when the bonds of the products form. Which is correct?",["ΔH = −50 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +50 kJ; endothermic","ΔH = +50 kJ; exothermic","ΔH = +2150 kJ; endothermic"],"B",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c8-bond-energy",0],["A student states that activation energy is the energy possessed by products. The correct interpretation is:",["Energy required to start a reaction","Total energy of products","Energy released during reaction","Energy stored in catalyst"],"A",3,0,"1afc2ff42103",1],["Mariam's mother cooks on a gas stove. The gas is methane, CH₄. It burns in oxygen to give carbon dioxide and water, and gives out heat. The gas does not start burning until a flame from a match is brought near it. On the energy profile for this reaction, arrow P goes up from the reactants to the top of the curve, and arrow Q goes from the reactants down to the products.\n\nWhat does arrow P show?",["The activation energy","The enthalpy change of the reaction","The energy of the products","The heat lost to the room"],"A",5,1,"254e094bbeac",1],["Mariam's mother cooks on a gas stove. The gas is methane, CH₄. It burns in oxygen to give carbon dioxide and water, and gives out heat. The gas does not start burning until a flame from a match is brought near it. On the energy profile for this reaction, arrow P goes up from the reactants to the top of the curve, and arrow Q goes from the reactants down to the products.\n\nArrow Q shows the enthalpy change. What is its sign for burning methane, and why?",["Positive, because heat is taken in","Negative, because the products have less energy than the reactants","Positive, because the curve goes up first","Zero, because energy is never lost"],"B",5,1,"70ff65bf4a84",1],["Mariam's mother cooks on a gas stove. The gas is methane, CH₄. It burns in oxygen to give carbon dioxide and water, and gives out heat. The gas does not start burning until a flame from a match is brought near it. On the energy profile for this reaction, arrow P goes up from the reactants to the top of the curve, and arrow Q goes from the reactants down to the products.\n\nWhy does the gas not start burning until a match is brought near?",["Methane only burns in the dark","The match adds more methane","The match makes the reaction endothermic","The match gives the particles enough energy to react"],"D",5,1,"645064d661fb",1],["Mariam's mother cooks on a gas stove. The gas is methane, CH₄. It burns in oxygen to give carbon dioxide and water, and gives out heat. The gas does not start burning until a flame from a match is brought near it. On the energy profile for this reaction, arrow P goes up from the reactants to the top of the curve, and arrow Q goes from the reactants down to the products.\n\nIf a catalyst could be used for this reaction, what would change in the diagram?",["Both arrows get shorter","Arrow Q gets longer, but arrow P stays the same","Arrow P gets shorter, but arrow Q stays the same","The products move above the reactants"],"C",5,1,"8af95d17f70d",1],["In a factory, hydrogen gas reacts with chlorine gas to make hydrogen chloride gas: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The table gives some bond energies. H–H is 436 kJ/mol. Cl–Cl is 243 kJ/mol. H–Cl is 432 kJ/mol. I–I is 151 kJ/mol. H–I is 299 kJ/mol.\n\nWhich bonds are broken when hydrogen reacts with chlorine?",["H–Cl bonds only","H–H and Cl–Cl bonds","H–H and H–Cl bonds","No bonds are broken"],"B",5,1,"c8de03e5aca4",1],["In a factory, hydrogen gas reacts with chlorine gas to make hydrogen chloride gas: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The table gives some bond energies. H–H is 436 kJ/mol. Cl–Cl is 243 kJ/mol. H–Cl is 432 kJ/mol. I–I is 151 kJ/mol. H–I is 299 kJ/mol.\n\nHow much energy is given out when the H–Cl bonds in 2 moles of HCl form?",["432 kJ","679 kJ","864 kJ","1296 kJ"],"C",5,1,"7a0105915b80",1],["In a factory, hydrogen gas reacts with chlorine gas to make hydrogen chloride gas: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The table gives some bond energies. H–H is 436 kJ/mol. Cl–Cl is 243 kJ/mol. H–Cl is 432 kJ/mol. I–I is 151 kJ/mol. H–I is 299 kJ/mol.\n\nWhat is the enthalpy change, ΔH, for H₂ + I₂ → 2HI?",["+185 kJ/mol","-11 kJ/mol","+1543 kJ/mol","-185 kJ/mol"],"B",5,1,"e4cfdf6d5077",1],["In a factory, hydrogen gas reacts with chlorine gas to make hydrogen chloride gas: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The table gives some bond energies. H–H is 436 kJ/mol. Cl–Cl is 243 kJ/mol. H–Cl is 432 kJ/mol. I–I is 151 kJ/mol. H–I is 299 kJ/mol.\n\nUse the table to compare this reaction with H₂ + I₂ → 2HI. Which statement is correct?",["Making HCl gives out much more heat than making HI","Making HI gives out more heat than making HCl","Both reactions take in heat","Both reactions give out exactly the same heat"],"A",5,1,"6a0d6f8123ee",1],["A student investigates a chemical reaction in which the reactants have an enthalpy of 250 kJ mol⁻¹ and the products have an enthalpy of 150 kJ mol⁻¹. The uncatalyzed reaction reaches a maximum energy of 450 kJ mol⁻¹. A catalyst provides an alternative pathway whose maximum energy is 350 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nWhat is the enthalpy change (ΔH) of the reaction?",["+100 kJ mol⁻¹","−100 kJ mol⁻¹","+400 kJ mol⁻¹","−400 kJ mol⁻¹"],"B",5,1,"682c647806e4",1],["A student investigates a chemical reaction in which the reactants have an enthalpy of 250 kJ mol⁻¹ and the products have an enthalpy of 150 kJ mol⁻¹. The uncatalyzed reaction reaches a maximum energy of 450 kJ mol⁻¹. A catalyst provides an alternative pathway whose maximum energy is 350 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nThe reaction is:",["Endothermic because energy is required for activation","Exothermic because the products have lower enthalpy than the reactants","Endothermic because a catalyst is used","Neither exothermic nor endothermic"],"B",5,1,"e95cb987d765",1],["A student investigates a chemical reaction in which the reactants have an enthalpy of 250 kJ mol⁻¹ and the products have an enthalpy of 150 kJ mol⁻¹. The uncatalyzed reaction reaches a maximum energy of 450 kJ mol⁻¹. A catalyst provides an alternative pathway whose maximum energy is 350 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nWhat is the activation energy of the uncatalyzed reaction?",["100 kJ mol⁻¹","200 kJ mol⁻¹","250 kJ mol⁻¹","450 kJ mol⁻¹"],"B",5,1,"6dff9f08c28f",1],["A student investigates a chemical reaction in which the reactants have an enthalpy of 250 kJ mol⁻¹ and the products have an enthalpy of 150 kJ mol⁻¹. The uncatalyzed reaction reaches a maximum energy of 450 kJ mol⁻¹. A catalyst provides an alternative pathway whose maximum energy is 350 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nWhat is the activation energy when the catalyst is used?",["50 kJ mol⁻¹","100 kJ mol⁻¹","200 kJ mol⁻¹","350 kJ mol⁻¹"],"B",5,1,"570fd75ab3d2",1],["A student investigates a chemical reaction in which the reactants have an enthalpy of 250 kJ mol⁻¹ and the products have an enthalpy of 150 kJ mol⁻¹. The uncatalyzed reaction reaches a maximum energy of 450 kJ mol⁻¹. A catalyst provides an alternative pathway whose maximum energy is 350 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nWhich statement correctly describes the effect of the catalyst?",["It decreases ΔH from −100 to −200 kJ mol⁻¹","It increases the enthalpy of the products","It decreases activation energy without changing the overall ΔH","It changes the reaction from exothermic to endothermic"],"C",5,1,"93943cb43643",1],["A student studies the reaction H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The average bond energies are: H–H = 436 kJ mol⁻¹, Cl–Cl = 242 kJ mol⁻¹, H–Cl = 431 kJ mol⁻¹. The student knows that breaking bonds requires energy, whereas forming bonds releases energy.\n\nHow much energy is required to break all the bonds in the reactants?",["194 kJ mol⁻¹","436 kJ mol⁻¹","678 kJ mol⁻¹","862 kJ mol⁻¹"],"C",5,1,"79ff7e139390",1],["A student studies the reaction H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The average bond energies are: H–H = 436 kJ mol⁻¹, Cl–Cl = 242 kJ mol⁻¹, H–Cl = 431 kJ mol⁻¹. The student knows that breaking bonds requires energy, whereas forming bonds releases energy.\n\nHow much energy is released when the H–Cl bonds are formed?",["431 kJ mol⁻¹","678 kJ mol⁻¹","862 kJ mol⁻¹","1293 kJ mol⁻¹"],"C",5,1,"53788aad2dab",1],["A student studies the reaction H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The average bond energies are: H–H = 436 kJ mol⁻¹, Cl–Cl = 242 kJ mol⁻¹, H–Cl = 431 kJ mol⁻¹. The student knows that breaking bonds requires energy, whereas forming bonds releases energy.\n\nUsing ΔH = energy absorbed − energy released, the approximate ΔH is:",["+184 kJ mol⁻¹","−184 kJ mol⁻¹","+1540 kJ mol⁻¹","−1540 kJ mol⁻¹"],"B",5,1,"f1da65bc93e0",1],["A student studies the reaction H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The average bond energies are: H–H = 436 kJ mol⁻¹, Cl–Cl = 242 kJ mol⁻¹, H–Cl = 431 kJ mol⁻¹. The student knows that breaking bonds requires energy, whereas forming bonds releases energy.\n\nBased on the calculated ΔH, the reaction is:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Thermally neutral","Impossible to classify"],"B",5,1,"905b8ad6fde8",1],["A student studies the reaction H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The average bond energies are: H–H = 436 kJ mol⁻¹, Cl–Cl = 242 kJ mol⁻¹, H–Cl = 431 kJ mol⁻¹. The student knows that breaking bonds requires energy, whereas forming bonds releases energy.\n\nIf the H–Cl bond energy were significantly lower while the other bond energies remained unchanged, the reaction would most likely:",["Become more exothermic","Become less exothermic or possibly endothermic","Have exactly the same ΔH","Require no activation energy"],"B",5,1,"ba011d906acb",1]]$j$::jsonb, true);
